Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.50,0:00:03.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This paper, G2N2, presented at ICLR 2024,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.25,0:00:09.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}asks a deceptively simple question: can we design a graph neural network whose expressive
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.22,0:00:11.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}power is guaranteed by construction,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.64,0:00:13.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}rather than proved after the fact?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.92,0:00:15.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The authors give a recipe.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.66,0:00:22.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}They take a fragment of an algebraic matrix language known to match the third-order Weisfeiler-Lehman test,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.84,0:00:28.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}write it as a context-free grammar, prune the grammar down to its essential rules,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.34,0:00:33.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and then translate those rules directly into the layers of a neural network.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.44,0:00:38.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The result is a provably three-W-L graph neural network that is both principled and,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.07,0:00:42.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}in practice, faster and more accurate than its competitors.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.33,0:00:45.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}How expressive is a graph neural network?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.97,0:00:50.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For years the field has answered that with the Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.81,0:00:55.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the gold standard has been to design a model and then prove it matches, say,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.96,0:00:57.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the third-order test.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.31,0:01:01.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But that proof comes after the design, almost as an afterthought.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.50,0:01:07.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}What has been missing is a systematic way to go the other direction: to start from a language
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.50,0:01:10.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}we already know is exactly as powerful as 3-W-L,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.59,0:01:14.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and mechanically build a network that inherits that power.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.33,0:01:19.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Without such a recipe, every expressive architecture is a fresh, hand-crafted proof.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.04,0:01:25.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The seed of the idea comes from a groundbreaking observation: the 1-W-L and 3-W-L tests
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.93,0:01:30.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}can each be rewritten as a fragment of a matrix language called MATLANG.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.80,0:01:37.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Two graphs look the same to 3-W-L if and only if every sentence you can write in the fragment
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.10,0:01:39.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}ML-of-L-three gives them the same value.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.80,0:01:43.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}That is a beautiful bridge between combinatorics and algebra.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.93,0:01:45.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But a bridge is not a road.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.76,0:01:48.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Turning one of these fragments into an actual,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.87,0:01:52.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}trainable network had been done only case by case,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.26,0:01:56.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the resulting models could not claim the full 3-W-L guarantee.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.72,0:02:00.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The motivation here is to pave that road once and for all.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:00.95,0:02:04.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper makes four contributions.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.72,0:02:08.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.49,0:02:12.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a generic framework that turns any fragment of an algebraic language into a graph neural
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.26,0:02:15.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}network through context-free grammars.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.03,0:02:19.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second, it runs that framework on the ML-of-L-three fragment and out comes G2N2,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.80,0:02:23.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a network that is provably 3-W-L.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:23.57,0:02:27.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Third, it validates the rule set experimentally,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.34,0:02:31.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}showing that the grammar reduction keeps expressiveness while trimming redundancy.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.11,0:02:34.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And fourth, across a broad battery of downstream tasks,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.88,0:02:38.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}G2N2 beats the existing 3-W-L networks, often while running faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:38.95,0:02:41.21,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Here is the recipe in three moves.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.29,0:02:47.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Start from the operation set L-three: matrix product, transpose, the all-ones vector,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.14,0:02:49.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}diagonal, and element-wise product.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.55,0:02:55.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Write down an exhaustive grammar whose sentences are exactly the fragment ML-of-L-three.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.60,0:02:57.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Then reduce that grammar,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.32,0:03:03.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}stripping away redundant rules and variables until only the essential productions remain,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.45,0:03:06.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}while proving each step keeps the 3-W-L guarantee.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.89,0:03:12.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Now the magic: the surviving variables tell you what the network's inputs should be,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.67,0:03:16.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and each surviving rule becomes a piece of a layer.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.18,0:03:20.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Concretely, a layer carries an edge memory C and a node memory H;
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.65,0:03:24.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}learnable linear blocks combine slices of these tensors,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.51,0:03:28.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the reduced rules like M-times-M and M-Hadamard-M are computed,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.84,0:03:32.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and two small MLPs stitch everything back together.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.35,0:03:37.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Stack these layers, add permutation-equivariant readouts, and you have G2N2.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.88,0:03:41.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The evaluation spans three very different arenas.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.36,0:03:46.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For regression, the QM9 dataset of one hundred thirty thousand small molecules,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.98,0:03:51.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}with twelve quantum-chemical targets, including R-squared,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:51.10,0:03:52.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the hardest one to predict.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.02,0:03:56.21,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For classification, the classic TUD benchmark,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.29,0:04:02.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}six datasets ranging from molecules like MUTAG and PTC to social graphs like IMDB.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.12,0:04:04.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And for a spectral stress test,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.33,0:04:12.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a node-regression task on nine-hundred-node graphs that asks whether the model can act as a band-pass filter.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.08,0:04:14.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Together they probe accuracy, generality,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.99,0:04:19.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and a subtle spectral ability that trips up other 3-W-L models.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.77,0:04:24.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The headline result is that G2N2 does not just match the theory,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.12,0:04:25.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it dominates in practice.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.82,0:04:28.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On QM9, learning targets one at a time,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.47,0:04:33.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it posts the best error on every single target while training faster than PPGN.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.83,0:04:36.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On the notoriously hard R-squared target,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.62,0:04:39.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}its error drops to zero-point-three-four-two,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.68,0:04:44.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}where PPGN sits at three-point-seven-eight, more than a ten-fold improvement,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.91,0:04:49.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and when all twelve targets are learned at once the gap widens further.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:49.73,0:04:55.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On graph classification it beats the second-best network on five of the six TUD datasets.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.78,0:05:00.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And on the spectral test it cleanly learns band-pass filters where PPGN,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.67,0:05:04.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}starved of the memory it would need, essentially fails.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:04.71,0:05:08.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The most instructive experiment is the grammar-reduction ablation.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.93,0:05:12.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The authors compare the full grammar, an intermediate one,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.64,0:05:16.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the reduced grammar r-G-of-L-three on the QM9 R-squared target.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.93,0:05:19.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Their errors are essentially the same,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:19.36,0:05:24.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which confirms that reduction throws away redundancy without touching expressive power.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.92,0:05:29.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But when you push past the reduced grammar and start deleting essential rules,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:29.91,0:05:32.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}performance degrades in a measurable way.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:32.54,0:05:38.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}That degradation is actually useful information: it tells you how much each operation contributes,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.80,0:05:44.61,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so you can prune the model deliberately when a task does not demand the full 3-W-L strength.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:44.99,0:05:47.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}A few numbers capture the impact.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.24,0:05:48.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On QM9's R-squared target,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.01,0:05:53.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}error falls from three-point-seven-eight to zero-point-three-four-two,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:53.78,0:05:55.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}roughly an eleven-fold reduction,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:56.03,0:06:02.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the model does it in ninety-eight seconds per epoch versus PPGN's one hundred twenty-nine.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.44,0:06:04.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On the spectral band-pass task,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.55,0:06:10.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}its R-squared score of zero-point-eight-two towers over PPGN's zero-point-one-zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:10.21,0:06:16.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And on the TUD classification suite it lands better than second place on five of six datasets,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:16.62,0:06:19.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}including ninety-two-and-a-half percent on MUTAG.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:20.26,0:06:22.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The lasting message is a change of workflow.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:23.07,0:06:28.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Instead of designing a graph network and then hoping to prove it is expressive,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:28.13,0:06:32.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}you can start from a language whose expressive power you already know,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.60,0:06:37.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reduce it to a clean grammar, and read the network straight off the rules,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.34,0:06:39.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}expressiveness guaranteed by construction.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:40.02,0:06:45.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}G2N2 is the concrete payoff of that idea: a provably 3-W-L model that is faster and more
Dialogue: 0,0:06:45.65,0:06:47.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}accurate than its predecessors.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:47.63,0:06:49.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And because the framework is generic,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.00,0:06:55.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the same grammatical route could turn other algebraic fragments into other networks,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.37,0:06:58.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}each carrying its expressive power by design.
